          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is there a real difference between 'elevation' and 'facade'?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Technically yes. Elevation refers to the drawing or the view of one side of a building, while facade refers more specifically to the physical, built exterior skin. In everyday conversation the two are used interchangeably, and when a homeowner searches for elevation design for a house in India, they are almost always asking about the front facade, since that is the elevation that gets designed, discussed and photographed the most."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I choose the right material for my house elevation?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Weigh four factors against each other: upfront cost, expected lifespan, maintenance requirement, and how the material performs in your specific regional climate. Plaster and texture paint cost the least but need repainting every four to six years. Stone and tile cladding are durable but heavy and labour intensive. Fibre cement panels sit in the mid-range. Engineered metal facade systems cost more upfront but offer the strongest combination of design flexibility, coating warranty and long-term durability."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Vastu Shastra conflict with modern elevation materials like metal?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In practice, no. Vastu guidance on entrances, orientation and material weight is generally compatible with good elevation design, since both favour a clearly defined entrance and visual grounding at the base of the building. Confusion often arises around specific materials, including metal, being assumed to conflict with certain directions, but placement, proportion and colour matter far more than the base material itself. Homeowners with specific Vastu requirements should raise them at the design brief stage."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I hire an architect or a facade company for my elevation?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a new build, an architect typically leads the overall design, including the elevation concept, and may bring in a specialist facade company to execute a specific cladding system. For a renovation or facade-only upgrade on an existing structure, many homeowners work directly with a facade specialist since the structural design is already fixed. Either path works, but it is worth clarifying early who owns final responsibility for waterproofing and substructure detailing."
          }
        }
